WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.970
- I know what you're thinking.

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:05.290
In Defense of Auto-Tune,
what is there to defend?

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.570
Sure, we may think we
know how Auto-Tune works,

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:11.130
and it's practically an
industry staple these days,

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.540
but there's more to it

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.300
than making someone sound like a robot.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.560
- So you're singing over a piano sample

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.940
I recorded in F sharp minor.
- Okay.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:22.580
- And so once you sing, we'll
have your vocals preserved,

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.090
there's no Auto-Tune running through it,

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.780
and then I'll apply the
Auto-Tune and we'll see,

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:27.810
compare how it sounds.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.640
I am Elijah Fox, I am a pianist,
producer, and songwriter.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.680
I'm based in Los Angeles right now.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.400
Me and Linda both met
at Oberlin Conservatory.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.371
- Yes.
- Where we graduated in 2017.

00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:42.150
♪ Na na na na na na ♪

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.520
- [Narrator] The use of
Auto-Tune has become so common

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.330
since it launched in the late '90s,

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:47.930
that it's easy to forget that it caught

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.510
so much flack throughout the 2000s.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.870
Many were concerned that it
was making music inauthentic.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.420
- In this video, we're gonna
talk about how Auto-Tune

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.250
became the in-demand tool
for recording studios,

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.570
helped artists find their artistic selves,

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.270
and evolved popular music.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:04.750
And be sure to stay for the end

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.900
where we will create an
original track using Auto-Tune.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.159
This is "In Defense of Auto-Tune."

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:13.171
♪ Like summer in the fall ♪

00:01:13.171 --> 00:01:17.950
♪ I shouldn't have cared at all ♪

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:21.070
- Auto-Tune was first released in 1997,

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:23.991
but let's first set the stage
for pop music at that time.

00:01:23.991 --> 00:01:25.960
(upbeat music)

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.110
Let's go back to the 1980s,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.700
the decade that saw an
increase in recording studios

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.540
using digital recording and
non-traditional instruments

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.160
like drum machines and synthesizers,

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.763
and let's not forget the birth of MTV.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.700
- [Narrator] All of these
things helped to transform

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.060
pop music into what we know it as today.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.060
With artists like
Madonna, Whitney Houston,

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.870
Michael Jackson, and Prince

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.690
serving as inspiration for future stars.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:55.020
- Pop stars were becoming larger than life

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.330
and their performances
had to reflect that.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.950
Choreographed dance
routines, wardrobe changes.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.580
Pop artists were now expected

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.080
to put on full productions
with their music.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.570
So lip syncing became more
common in arena concerts

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.240
in the late '80s and onward.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:11.860
- Well girl, you know it's True,

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.410
it can only mean Milli Vanilli.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.553
- This all came to ahead in 1989

00:02:16.553 --> 00:02:19.347
when Milli Vanilli were
performing their hit single

00:02:19.347 --> 00:02:21.340
"Girl You Know It's True" live

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.160
and the chorus of the
track kept repeating.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:24.281
♪ Girl you know it's ♪

00:02:24.281 --> 00:02:25.417
♪ Girl you know it's ♪

00:02:25.417 --> 00:02:26.250
♪ Girl you know it's ♪

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.480
- The Milli Vanilli controversy,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.100
as well as general pushback
against artists lip syncing

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.500
during live performances

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.070
played a part in why Auto-Tune
wasn't widely promoted

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:35.290
when it first came out.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.500
Something its own inventor noticed, too,

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.387
sharing in a 2016 article,

00:02:39.387 --> 00:02:42.060
"Studios weren't going
out and advertising,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.890
'Hey, we got Auto-Tune!'

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.740
Back then, the public
was weary of the idea

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.320
of fake or affected music.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.340
What they don't understand is
that the method used before,

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.310
doing hundreds of takes
and splicing them together,

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.120
was its own form of
artificial pitch correction."

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.040
Those are the words of Andy Hildebrand,

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.200
an engineer, a mathematician,
a former flute player,

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.110
and of course, the man behind Auto-Tune.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.510
- [Narrator] Launched in September, 1997,

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.620
Auto-Tune was the result of Hildebrand

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:13.630
fusing math with music,

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.410
with some inspiration from a colleague

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:17.970
who joked about wanting a
device to help her sing in tune.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:21.340
- What Auto-Tune does is
it automatically corrects

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:23.820
the note you're singing

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.940
towards the closest version of that note

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.920
that's tuned to A440 or standard tuning.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:35.130
So that means if you sing a
note that's close to a D sharp,

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.960
it's gonna move it down to a D sharp

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.680
'cause that's in that key.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.400
But if you sing a note that's in-between,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.920
it's gonna have to pick
whatever you were closest to

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.920
and then auto-tune it to
the notes from that scale.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.800
- Cher's "Believe" is the song

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.180
that made Auto-Tune known
throughout the world,

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.340
but not for the pitch correcting
reason it was made for.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.888
Notice how Cher's voice
sounds inhuman, robotic-like.

00:03:59.888 --> 00:04:04.888
♪ Ooh, do you believe in life after love ♪

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:09.010
- This is because of Auto-Tune's
retune speed feature.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:13.680
Can you explain what the
retune speed function is

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.710
and what it does?

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:16.543
- Definitely.

00:04:16.543 --> 00:04:20.680
Retune speed is basically on
the interface of the Auto-Tune,

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.100
there's a Retune button,
which is basically telling

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.870
the software how fast you
want the notes to transition

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:29.530
while it's kind of reading your vocals.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:31.410
So to put that kind of more easier,

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.100
it's just like you can
go from a retune speed,

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.230
which is really low,
which is literally zero.

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:40.107
Or you can go to a higher
retune speed of 50, 70,

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.930
where you probably wouldn't
even detect it at all.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:46.166
- [Elijah] This is the
vocal completely dry.

00:04:46.166 --> 00:04:47.823
♪ Ooh, na na na na na na ♪

00:04:47.823 --> 00:04:49.401
- [Elijah] So I'll put an Auto-Tune.

00:04:49.401 --> 00:04:51.492
♪ Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah ♪

00:04:51.492 --> 00:04:53.190
- [Elijah] And let's see what it does.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.745
Here we go.

00:04:54.745 --> 00:04:58.290
♪ It rains, it rains so rarely here ♪

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.380
- So it sounds absolutely terrible

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.330
because we haven't set the key yet.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:06.600
So here I'm gonna go F sharp, scale minor,

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.780
then I'm gonna put it so that
the retune speed is slow.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.290
So this will be the most realistic.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:14.160
And then I'm gonna set
humanize to the max.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.323
So now we've got

00:05:15.323 --> 00:05:18.028
♪ Ooh na na na na na ♪

00:05:18.028 --> 00:05:21.140
- [Elijah] And you can see
the notes that Linda's singing

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:22.780
are showing up here.

00:05:24.068 --> 00:05:24.901
♪ It rains, it rains ♪

00:05:24.901 --> 00:05:28.926
- [Elijah] So if I do retune
speed fast, it's that classic.

00:05:28.926 --> 00:05:32.880
♪ But in my mind the
clouds don't ever clear ♪

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.890
- Auto-Tune could not only
be used to correct notes.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:38.270
It could also be used as a creative tool

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:40.810
to change one's voice entirely.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:44.120
But the use of Auto-Tune
wasn't welcomed by all.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.813
- At what point did you see

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:49.530
that Auto-Tune was like, oh,
this is tight, this is cool.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:53.480
Like who was the artist
that turned it on for you?

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:54.910
- Well, definitely I'm not gonna lie,

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:58.480
the first artist that
really turned it on for me

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.570
was back in the day watching
"106 &amp; Park", T-Pain.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.770
As soon as his music came on,

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:06.320
it just was something totally...

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:11.204
At least for people of my
age and it was pretty new.

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:13.761
♪ I like the bartender ♪

00:06:13.761 --> 00:06:15.740
♪ Yeah, if you're lookin' for me ♪

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:18.050
- From the mid to late 2000s,

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:21.370
T-Pain became synonymous with Auto-Tune.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:24.890
He popularized the effect in R&amp;B and Rap,

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:28.250
giving way to hit singles
like Lil Wayne's "Lollipop"

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:31.493
and Kanye West's "808s
&amp; Heartbreak" album.

00:06:31.493 --> 00:06:34.190
♪ How could you be so heartless ♪

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:35.023
♪ How could you be so heartless ♪

00:06:35.023 --> 00:06:36.300
- Auto-Tune became more well-known

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.690
for distorting vocals than fixing them.

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:41.710
With auto-tuned hits like Black
Eyed Peas' "Boom Boom Pow"

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:43.000
and Kesha's "Tik Tok"

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.364
being some of the decade's biggest songs.

00:06:45.364 --> 00:06:47.280
♪ Tick-tock on the clock ♪

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.103
♪ But the party don't stop, no ♪

00:06:49.103 --> 00:06:51.920
- Because of this, the
tool became a target

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.370
by those who thought it
was making music artificial

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:57.320
and fake, like the lip-sync
debacle of the 90s,

00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:59.920
Auto-Tune was facing a similar moment.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:02.400
- Jay Z called for Auto-Tune's death

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.940
on his 2009 song, "D.O.A."
"Death of Auto-Tune,"

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.281
where he condemned the
overuse of the effect.

00:07:09.281 --> 00:07:12.513
♪ This is anti auto-tune,
death of the ring-tone ♪

00:07:12.513 --> 00:07:13.346
♪ This ain't for iTunes ♪

00:07:13.346 --> 00:07:14.500
- Although Jay clarified

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:18.410
that the song wasn't directed
at T-Pain, West, or Wayne,

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:21.530
who we all saw as good
examples of using Auto-Tune,

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:23.300
T-Pain took it personally.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:24.930
In an interview with Netflix,

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:26.780
T-Pain recounts a time where Usher

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.950
told him that he ruined music.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:30.260
- [Narrator] That is the very moment,

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:33.070
and I don't even think I
realized this for a long time,

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:34.200
but that's the very moment

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:37.444
that started a four-year
depression for me."

00:07:37.444 --> 00:07:39.480
- [Caller] Hey man, look
you're well missed, man.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.780
I wish you would have never disappeared.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.000
You disappeared when Jay Z
came out with damn record.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.100
I wish you would've stayed,
you made good quality music.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:50.170
- Well, the flack directed
at Auto-Tune and T-Pain

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:51.260
didn't age well.

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:54.290
For starters, the artist is
actually a very good singer

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:57.353
as he showcased in his
2014 Tiny Desk performance.

00:07:57.353 --> 00:08:01.055
♪ Yeah, ooh ooh ohh, ooh ooh ♪

00:08:01.055 --> 00:08:02.997
♪ We in the bed like ♪

00:08:02.997 --> 00:08:05.160
♪ Na na na na na na na ♪

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.570
- And what you used is
not what Roger used.

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:10.070
- No, Roger used a vocoder.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:11.823
- What's that?
- Roger used a vocoder.

00:08:11.823 --> 00:08:15.280
- Vocoders use synthesizers
to alter a singer's voice

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.210
and musicians have been
using them since the 1950s.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:20.400
Listen to Herbie Hancock use a vocoder

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:23.545
in the 1978 track "I Thought It Was You."

00:08:23.545 --> 00:08:26.410
♪ Thought it was you ♪

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:28.870
♪ I thought it was you ♪

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:31.630
- Auto-Tune isn't just a
tool for pitch correcting,

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:34.510
but a spiritual successor
to previous inventions

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:36.320
used for vocal experimentation.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:39.190
Auto-Tune and similar
products like Melodyne

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:41.280
are still being used for pitch correcting

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:43.010
and vocal experimentation.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:45.210
Some producers even use them in tandem.

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:46.320
- Can you tell us the difference

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.840
between Auto-Tune and Melodyne
and like how you would use.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:50.673
- Yeah.

00:08:50.673 --> 00:08:53.530
So what Melodyne does is
it doesn't actually correct

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:55.950
any of the notes, but
it shows you how many

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:58.810
since you were sharp
or flat for each note,

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:00.640
and then lets you adjust.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.300
So, with Melodyne, you can
kind of go halfway sort of,

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:06.860
but Auto-Tune is just
gonna directly tune it

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:08.842
to standard tuning.

00:09:08.842 --> 00:09:10.850
(device beeping)

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:12.556
- Linda, what's up, how you doing?

00:09:12.556 --> 00:09:14.340
- Good, it's good to see you.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:15.620
- Yes, always great seeing you.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:18.040
So Auto-Tune (imitates Auto-Tune)

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:19.775
the effect.
- Yeah, yeah! (laughs)

00:09:19.775 --> 00:09:21.120
- I was always doing
that when I was a kid,

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.130
so I'm glad that we
spent some time with it.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.230
- Yeah, me too.

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:26.610
- How much experience do
you have with Auto-Tune?

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.080
- To be honest, I always
knew that it was like

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:32.730
different than what the general
public perceived it to be.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.610
And I think that's kind of
like, you know, for a long time,

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:37.060
the music industry was hiding the fact

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:38.950
that people even used Auto-Tune.

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:40.820
I think a lot of people
have a misconception

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:45.110
about like how vocalists use their voices

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.860
and about what Auto-Tune
necessarily means.

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:50.190
Like they think of it as like
slap the Auto-Tune filter

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:53.100
over everything you sing, and
that's Auto-Tune, you know?

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.490
And it's a lot more nuanced than that.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:57.340
I think sometimes people will watch

00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:59.560
these like live performances
and they'll be like,

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:01.720
see, this person doesn't
need any Auto-Tune,

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.360
and it's like, that mic was auto-tuned.

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:07.070
- People have no idea like
how much it's actually used

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:07.903
in the industry.

00:10:07.903 --> 00:10:10.470
- In LA, for every session that I was in,

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.640
the mic automatically had Auto-Tune on it,

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.850
and I had never experienced that, yeah.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:19.110
- So now it's time to apply
our own flavor to Auto-Tune,

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:21.680
and make an original
song using the effect.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:23.750
I think I'm ready to hear this track.

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:25.540
- [Linda] Yes, okay.

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:26.540
- Here goes nothing.

00:10:27.379 --> 00:10:32.079
♪ It rains, it rains so rarely here ♪

00:10:32.079 --> 00:10:33.640
♪ But in my mind ♪

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:35.210
- I'm thinking about what
I could do with this.

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:37.170
What I would do with it?

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:40.540
A boom-bap kind of thing,
it feels too forceful.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:42.537
I'm thinking like sparse samps.

00:10:43.515 --> 00:10:45.980
- Mm-hm.
- You know what I'm saying?

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:48.003
- Like little brushes.

00:10:48.003 --> 00:10:50.760
- Ah! I need that. (laughs)

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:52.291
- [Linda] That's what I was thinking.

00:10:52.291 --> 00:10:53.893
- I need that!

00:10:53.893 --> 00:10:54.921
No, let's do it!

00:10:54.921 --> 00:10:59.710
♪ It rains, it rains so rarely here ♪

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:04.710
♪ But in my mind the
clouds don't ever clear ♪

00:11:05.102 --> 00:11:10.102
♪ Without a smile, I am so out of place ♪

00:11:10.312 --> 00:11:15.312
♪ So I can try to put one on my face ♪

00:11:16.327 --> 00:11:21.327
♪ It feels like summer in the fall ♪

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:25.450
♪ I shouldn't have cared at all ♪

00:11:29.276 --> 00:11:30.109
- Hmm.

